{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZIsoformalInterface","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZIsoformalInterface","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Fin","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Order.Ring.Int","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":14,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.isoformalDim_eq_zero_of_pure","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.isoformalDim_eq_zero_of_pure","kind":"theorem","line":152,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L152-L157","doc":"The same conclusion for **any** rank and slope, so it cannot be read as an\nartefact of the particular numbers `(2, 1)`: what kills the Stokes data is `k = 1`\nalone. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.c1_infinity_isoformalDim_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.c1_infinity_isoformalDim_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L118-L150","doc":"**C1's `∞` has NO Stokes data — the instance, not just the general lemma.**\n\n`L1`'s Newton polygon at `∞` has a single edge `(r,d) = (2,1)`: slope `½` with\nmultiplicity `2`, pure isoclinic.  After the ramified pullback `ζ² = x` — which\n`QOU.QBeta.ramified_qBorel_budget` showed is the only pairing with sub-quadratic\nleftover — the data is `k = 1`, rank `2`, slope `1`.  The isoformal space is a\n**point**.\n\nSo there is no **local isoformal** q-Stokes multiplier at C1's `∞` to compute.\nIndependently: RSZ's Stokes matrices are unipotent automorphisms inducing the\nidentity on `gr M₀` (§4.4, Thm 5.5.7), and with one slope there is no\nstrictly-upper-triangular part — the Stokes group is trivial.\n\n**Do not read this as \"C1 has no Stokes multipliers\".**  Two different objects\nshare the name, and only the first is zero-dimensional:\n\n* *local isoformal moduli at `∞`* — `F(P₁,…,P_k)` at a single point.  **This\n  lemma. Dimension 0.**\n* *the connection-matrix Stokes multipliers* — the data in `P = Y₀⁻¹Y_∞` between\n  the theta-normalised solutions at `0` and at `∞`, functions of the invariant\n  `χ = P₀₀P₁₁/(P₀₁P₁₀)`.  **Global, live, and open** (bean `qou-wgnx`; the\n  connection matrix and `χ` are already built).  A dimension count at one point\n  says nothing about it.\n\n**This instance exists because the general lemma above was not enough.**\n`rszIsoformalDim_one`'s docstring already said \"no Stokes data to extract\", and I\nstill wrote \"the Stokes multiplier is the remaining open item\" three times across\nPRs #4771 and #4783.  A lemma naming C1 is harder to walk past than a lemma about\nan abstract `k = 1`.  Retraction:\n[`2026-08-08-c1-infinity-has-no-stokes-multiplier.md`](../../../../../docs/audits/2026-08-08-c1-infinity-has-no-stokes-multiplier.md). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszIsoformalDim_two","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszIsoformalDim_two","kind":"theorem","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L111-L116","doc":"Under §3.3's hypotheses the isoformal space of a *two*-slope class has the\nexpected dimension `r₀ r₁ (μ₁ − μ₀)` — the only case in which the \"`ℂ^d`\"\ndescription of the corpus could have applied. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.not_integralSlopeSetting_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.not_integralSlopeSetting_one","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L103-L109","doc":"**A single slope cannot satisfy §3.3's hypotheses** — mechanically. This is\nthe C1 situation: one edge in the Newton polygon at `∞`, hence `k = 1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.IntegralSlopeSetting.strictly_increasing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.IntegralSlopeSetting.strictly_increasing","kind":"theorem","line":98,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L98-L98","doc":"RSZ's `μ₁ < ⋯ < μ_k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.IntegralSlopeSetting.two_or_more","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.IntegralSlopeSetting.two_or_more","kind":"theorem","line":96,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L96-L96","doc":"§3.3 needs at least two slopes: the level `μ₂ − μ₁` presupposes a pair. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.IntegralSlopeSetting.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.IntegralSlopeSetting.mk","kind":"ctor","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L94-L94","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.IntegralSlopeSetting","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.IntegralSlopeSetting","kind":"class","line":88,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L88-L98","doc":"**The standing hypotheses of §3.3**, as a class carrying its actual content.\n\nDeliberately *not* an empty marker: `two_or_more` and `integral` are exactly the\nassumptions the C1 chain dropped, and `strictly_increasing` is RSZ's `μ₁ < ⋯ < μ_k`.\nA consumer that cites §3.3 takes this as an instance binder and thereby inherits\nall three in its own type. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszIsoformalDim_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszIsoformalDim_zero","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L83-L86","doc":"Degenerate case, for completeness: no slopes, no moduli. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszIsoformalDim_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszIsoformalDim_one","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L76-L81","doc":"**The fact that decides the C1 question.** With a single slope there are no\npairs `i < j`, so the isoformal space is a point: dimension `0`. A pure isoclinic\nmodule has no isoformal moduli, hence no Stokes data to extract. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszIsoformalDim","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszIsoformalDim","kind":"def","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L71-L74","doc":"**RSZ Thm 3.3.5.** `F(P₁,…,P_k)` is an affine space of dimension\n`∑_{i<j} rᵢ rⱼ (μⱼ − μᵢ)`. Stated as the dimension formula itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszLevel_translation_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszLevel_translation_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L65-L69","doc":"The level is *not* a function of one slope: it is translation-invariant, so\nknowing `μ₂` alone determines nothing. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszLevel_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszLevel_pos","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L61-L63","doc":"Consecutive slopes are strictly increasing, so the level is positive. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszLevel","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RSZ.rszLevel","kind":"def","line":53,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RSZIsoformalInterface.lean#L53-L59","doc":"**RSZ's level**, p.38: `d := μ₂ − μ₁`, the difference between two consecutive\nslopes of the filtration.\n\nThe arity is the point. This is a **binary** operation on slopes; there is no\n\"level of a slope\". A single slope cannot be supplied, so the misreading\n\"slope `½` ⇒ level `2`\" is not expressible here. ","signatureChunk":0}]}